.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NSHD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nshd
.Nd network configuration daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl bdv
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads the system's configuration for
.Xr nsh 8 ,
which asks it over a socket.
It is privilege separated.
The parent process, as root, binds the sockets and starts the children,
each but system as a user of its own, so that none may signal or reach
the files of a child of another role:
.Bl -tag -width "frontend"
.It relay
Runs as _nshrelay in a
.Xr chroot 2 ,
tells each child when it may start, and the parent which child a peer
says went wrong, and carries no request.
.It show
Four of them, each as _nshshow in a
.Xr chroot 2 ,
answer the read-only socket, with what anyone may see: interfaces,
routes, the ARP and neighbor tables, VLANs, bridges, kernel
statistics, the IPsec flows, the version, who is logged in and the
hostname, as facts the client renders.
A long answer holds up only the one show that makes it, and is made
only as the client reads it.
A show has no path to the others.
It is not
.Xr pledge 2 Ns d ,
as no promise has the interface ioctls it asks.
Each opens
.Pa /var/run/utmp
before its
.Xr chroot 2 .
.It frontend
Runs as _nshfront in a
.Xr chroot 2
and answers the read-write socket.
Each client is a session, which asks one thing at a time.
.It config
Runs as _nshconf in a
.Xr chroot 2 ,
holds the sessions, and asks system for what they need.
A session asks nothing but
.Cm enable
until it is enabled: by the enable secret, which config checks by its
hash, which system reads, or with none if none is set; or by the token
an enabled session of the same user's was given.
A session in
.Cm configure private
has a candidate of its own; the sessions in
.Cm configure
share one, and each sees what the others change in it.
config edits a candidate a line at a time, with nsh's own commands, as
they run in
.Cm configure private :
the running configuration, and what they read of the kernel, are those
of a snapshot system makes when a session enters configure or shows
its candidate.
.Cm configure exclusive
is
.Cm configure
while no other session changes the shared candidate or commits any; it
is refused while another session has it, or while another user's
changes to the shared candidate are not committed, and leaving it, or
its session going, discards what it did not commit.
While a commit of the shared candidate runs, no other session changes
it.
A commit is one at a time, of all the sessions':
config plans it from a snapshot and checks it, has system test the
daemons' rules it changes, and has system record it, run its script,
and, if a snapshot after reads as the candidate, save what runs; if
not, system runs the script back.
.Cm commit check
stops after the tests.
.Cm commit confirmed
runs its script even if the candidate is what runs, and, if a snapshot
after reads as the candidate, keeps what runs without saving it until
its deadline; a commit that saves confirms it.
.Cm rollback Ar N
takes configuration
.Ar N
from system.
A commit whose session goes runs to its end.
config is
.Xr pledge 2 Ns d
to stdio: it reads no kernel, opens no file and runs no program of its
own, and refuses a line that would.
.It system
Runs as root, and reads the configuration from the kernel, the
daemons and the saved configuration, which it alone writes, in
.Pa /var/db/nsh ,
where
.Xr nsh 8 Ns 's
own
.Pa /etc/nshrc
is not; nsh's
.Cm show startup-config
asks for it.
It answers config and no one else.
What a daemon's control program prints for a show, and the DHCP
leases, a child of its own prints, and system streams it only as fast
as the client reads it; the child, and its process group, are killed
if the client goes first.
The program is the one nsh's tables name for the show, never one a
client names, and the child runs it as _nshcheck, unless system names
it as one that keeps root: a daemon's control program, as its control
socket,
.Pa /dev/pf
and PF_KEY are root's.
The child that prints the leases opens them, then becomes _nshcheck.
A commit's script, its save, and each test of a daemon's rules run in a
child of system's; one that runs past its time, 900 seconds for a
script, 300 for a save and 120 for a test, is sent SIGTERM, with its
process group, and SIGKILL 10 seconds later, 5 for a test, and fails.
A script and a save run as root.
A test runs as _nshcheck, with its rules in a directory of their own,
both _nshcheck's, but for relayd's, ldapd's, npppd's, sasyncd's and
sshd's, which read keys or secrets their rules name, and keep root.
While one runs, other requests wait, but streams go on.
system keeps a record of a commit that has begun, which a commit takes
away when it saves or goes back; one that does not leaves it, and the
next commit says what it left.
While the record says a commit's changes were not saved, a commit
that neither saves what runs nor goes back to before it is refused.
A commit confirmed makes a record of a commit not confirmed too, which
goes back to what ran before the first commit not confirmed; it tells
the parent, and goes on once the parent keeps its deadline too.
If the commit goes back, so does that record, to how it was before it.
A commit that saves takes that record away before it saves.
Its deadline is its minutes from when it is kept.
Past its deadline, system takes the commit back, once no commit it
began and no script, save or test is in the way: it demotes carp by
128, runs a script back, and promotes carp again once what runs reads
as it was.
A script back that fails is run again 5 seconds later, three times at
most; then the record says it failed, and carp stays demoted until a
commit saves.
A system that starts takes back what one before it did not.
A commit is refused if what runs is not what it was planned from.
system is a session of its own: when it goes, the parent kills every
process left in that session, and every process of _nshcheck's,
before another system starts.
If one outlives SIGKILL, no other system starts.
.It archive
Runs as _nsharc in a
.Xr chroot 2 ,
and keeps the shared candidate, and who changed it, for config, in
.Pa /var/db/nsh/archive ,
which it opens before it enters the
.Xr chroot 2 .
config sends it each time it changes, and answers the session that
changed it once archive has kept it, whole and synced to disk, or said
it did not; a config that starts takes what archive kept before a
session enters
.Cm configure .
If archive is down, a change is said not to be kept.
.El
.Pp
frontend and config, config and system, and config and archive, each
talk on a pipe of their own, which the parent makes, so each knows who
sent what it reads.
A child takes a peer's pipe from the parent alone.
Each child also has a pipe to relay.
The parent reads only what relay tells it, and what system says of a
commit not confirmed.
A child that exits, or sends relay or a peer what it may not, is
started again, with new pipes to its peers, and they forget what it
held: its sessions, or the answers it had not yet given.
A child that fails five times in a minute stays down.
Then the others answer for it, or, for frontend or the last show to
stay down, its socket refuses connections.
If relay fails, every child starts again with it; if relay fails five
times in a minute,
.Nm
exits.
.Pp
A commit not confirmed has a record, which the parent reads too.
If system has not taken the commit back 30 seconds past its deadline,
or past when system said what it does first may end, the parent starts
system again, which takes it back; three times at most.
While there is one, system stays up though its failures would keep it
down, for ten starts more.
.Pp
With
.Fl b ,
as rc starts it,
.Nm
boots: before frontend and the shows take clients, config has system
make the running configuration the saved one, all of it that takes, as
.Xr nsh 8
.Fl i
does; what check finds is said, and a daemon's rules are tested, but
neither keeps it from running.
What config and system print for it is logged.
.Nm
returns once the boot is done, with 0 if what runs is then the saved
configuration, else 1.
The first time, with no saved configuration, system takes
.Pa /etc/nshrc
as it.
system keeps a record of the boot, which names the boot of the host's
it is for; an
.Nm
started again in the same boot of the host's boots again only if the
boot did not end or did not go well.
carp is demoted by 128 until the boot goes well, or, if it does not,
until a commit saves.
A boot that goes well takes away a record of a commit not confirmed,
as what it would go back to runs.
If system or config starts again during the boot, the boot starts
again; if system stays down, the boot did not go well.
.Pp
Each child's
.Xr chroot 2
is its user's home directory.
.Nm
does not start unless each user, and _nshcheck, exists, is not root,
and has a uid no other has.
.Pp
Only root and the members of the group nshadmin may connect to the
read-write socket, and every session there may see the running
configuration.
The read-only socket is for anyone.
.Pp
system does as root what config asks of it, so a config gone wrong may
ask it anything a session may: config is trusted as the read-write
socket's users are.
relay carries no request; gone wrong, it can stop the children, or have
them started again, and nothing more.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl b
Boot, as above.
.It Fl d
Do not daemonize, and log to standard error.
.It Fl v
Produce more verbose output.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/run/nshd.ro.sockXX" -compact
.It Pa /var/run/nshd.sock
the read-write socket, mode 0660, group nshadmin
.It Pa /var/run/nshd.ro.sock
the read-only socket, mode 0666
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/nshrc
the saved configuration, root's
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/nshrc.N
the configurations saved before it, rollback 0 the last
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/commit
the commit record
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/confirm
the record of a commit not confirmed
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/boot
the record of the boot
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/archive/candidate
the shared candidate, as archive keeps it
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nsh 8
